{{/* signin is the page of a browser that sind ui refuses with 403 when it
opens a page: it is not signed in, or its link holds an old token, as after
sind ui started again. .Message is why; the page tells how to get the
address, and never the token. It has no layout, as a refused browser may
see no data, but it uses the static files, which need no session. */}}
{{define "signin"}}<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">
<title>Sign in · sind ui</title>
<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="{{call .Asset "icon.svg"}}">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{call .Asset "app.css"}}">
<script src="{{call .Asset "app.js"}}"></script>
</head>
<body>
<main class="signin" id="main">
  <div class="brand"><img src="{{call .Asset "icon.svg"}}" alt="" width="24" height="24">sind <small>ui</small></div>
  <h1>Open sind ui from its address</h1>
  <p class="callout warn">{{.Message}}</p>
  <p>sind ui lets in only the browser that opened the address it printed. That address holds a token, which is new each time sind ui starts. So after a restart, an open tab or a bookmark needs the new address.</p>
  <h2>Get the address</h2>
  <ol class="signin-steps">
    <li><span>In a terminal on the machine that runs sind ui, run:</span>
      <div class="cmd"><code>sind ui</code><button type="button" data-shell-copy="sind ui">Copy</button></div>
      <span class="muted">If sind ui runs, this prints its address. If not, it starts sind ui and prints the new one.</span></li>
    <li><span>Open the address that it prints, here or on another device, such as a phone or a laptop with the port forwarded.</span></li>
  </ol>
</main>
</body>
</html>
{{end}}
